WEBVTT

00:00:00.910 --> 00:00:02.940
Cleaning's all done!

00:00:03.240 --> 00:00:05.700
Okay, let's go, Miracchi, Makki!

00:00:05.700 --> 00:00:06.440
Yeah.

00:00:07.120 --> 00:00:10.220
Oh, I forgot my phone. 
You two go on ahead.

00:00:10.220 --> 00:00:11.780
Okay, okay.

00:00:15.690 --> 00:00:17.160
Oh, Ino-senpai.

00:00:17.600 --> 00:00:21.870
<b>Morning!</b>

00:00:19.120 --> 00:00:21.870
<i>Good morning! I'm glad it's sunny!</i>

00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:24.670
<i>All thanks to your teruteru bouzu dolls!</i>

00:00:27.350 --> 00:00:29.580
<i>We're all rooting for you!</i>

00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:40.830
Ao!

00:00:42.110 --> 00:00:43.360
Good morning!

00:00:43.650 --> 00:00:44.810
Good morning, Mira.

00:00:45.550 --> 00:00:46.790
It's sunny today, huh?

00:00:46.790 --> 00:00:47.810
Yeah!

00:00:49.080 --> 00:00:51.820
We might get to realize our dream today!

00:00:52.750 --> 00:00:55.140
Let's find your star, Ao!

00:02:26.540 --> 00:02:30.960
12. Connected Cosmos

00:02:31.670 --> 00:02:35.340
<i>After observing until morning, 
we entered day two of the Shiny Challenge.</i>

00:02:35.680 --> 00:02:37.800
<i>Today starts with our study session.</i>

00:02:38.260 --> 00:02:41.050
Okay, read the next page, Konohata-san.

00:02:41.050 --> 00:02:42.260
Okay.

00:02:42.760 --> 00:02:48.480
"Asteroids are small solar system bodies 
that form when rocks smash into planetoids.

00:02:48.850 --> 00:02:52.600
On the other hand, trans-Neptunian 
objects are said to be

00:02:49.360 --> 00:02:51.810
<i>Mira, you can do it!</i>

00:02:52.600 --> 00:02:56.540
ice leftover from the creation
 of the solar sausage..."

00:02:57.780 --> 00:02:59.080
Sausage?

00:03:02.070 --> 00:03:04.120
Let's take a break, shall we?

00:03:04.700 --> 00:03:07.610
"An asteroid's reflectance spectrum

00:03:07.610 --> 00:03:13.980
is derived from ordinary 
chondrite in S-types and"

00:03:13.980 --> 00:03:15.810
carbonaceous chondrite in C-types.

00:03:15.810 --> 00:03:16.900
What's that?

00:03:17.330 --> 00:03:19.940
When you analyze an asteroid's light,

00:03:19.940 --> 00:03:22.610
you can identify its general composition.

00:03:22.610 --> 00:03:26.390
Wow. So there are different 
types of asteroids, too?

00:03:26.820 --> 00:03:31.300
For example, the asteroid Hayabusa 
launched at was the stony S-type, Itokawa.

00:03:31.300 --> 00:03:35.520
The one that Hayabusa 2 was launched 
at was a carbonaceous C-type.

00:03:35.850 --> 00:03:40.830
Unlike planetoids, most asteroids 
haven't gone through thermal evolution.

00:03:40.830 --> 00:03:43.380
Meaning they're the same 
as they were long ago?

00:03:43.380 --> 00:03:45.520
Like cookies before they're baked?

00:03:45.520 --> 00:03:46.550
That's right.

00:03:46.550 --> 00:03:49.250
By studying asteroid rocks,

00:03:49.250 --> 00:03:51.980
we can trace the history of our solar system.

00:03:51.980 --> 00:03:53.330
I see...

00:03:54.290 --> 00:03:58.350
Going to space and bringing back rocks, huh?

00:03:58.350 --> 00:04:01.670
Sounds like a joint project between 
the astronomy and geology groups.

00:04:01.970 --> 00:04:04.140
You're in the earth sciences club, right?

00:04:04.140 --> 00:04:05.890
Why was it split into groups?

00:04:06.210 --> 00:04:07.660
Uh...

00:04:07.660 --> 00:04:10.230
Because it was formed by 
merging two different clubs.

00:04:10.230 --> 00:04:11.540
Huh.

00:04:11.540 --> 00:04:15.750
I guess it's true that there are a lot 
of fields lumped into earth sciences.

00:04:15.750 --> 00:04:17.650
It's literally "the science of Earth," right?

00:04:17.650 --> 00:04:20.670
Some call it geological sciences, too.

00:04:20.670 --> 00:04:21.860
Geoscience for short.

00:04:22.200 --> 00:04:23.500
Geoscience...

00:04:25.080 --> 00:04:26.580
Geoscience club...

00:04:29.530 --> 00:04:33.450
This should be all the reading we 
need to do for tomorrow's presentation.

00:04:33.450 --> 00:04:34.630
Right!

00:04:34.630 --> 00:04:38.040
All that's left now is to hope 
tonight's observation goes well!

00:04:39.390 --> 00:04:41.510
Looks like the weather will be okay tonight.

00:04:42.410 --> 00:04:43.260
Yeah.

00:04:48.970 --> 00:04:53.980
<b>Observation Control Room</b>

00:04:49.430 --> 00:04:53.200
Hanashima-san, Horiguchi-san, 
please take care of them.

00:04:53.200 --> 00:04:53.980
Sure.

00:04:54.500 --> 00:04:56.530
Well, good luck.

00:04:56.530 --> 00:04:58.750
And have fun!

00:04:58.750 --> 00:04:59.720
Okay!

00:05:00.640 --> 00:05:03.080
We'll begin photographing now.

00:05:03.080 --> 00:05:04.880
This will take a little time,

00:05:04.880 --> 00:05:08.530
so why don't you all go out and look 
at the stars while it's running?

00:05:08.840 --> 00:05:10.000
Can we?!

00:05:10.000 --> 00:05:10.740
Of course.

00:05:11.060 --> 00:05:14.480
There aren't many clouds out today,
so you should have a great view.

00:05:15.730 --> 00:05:18.310
We still have some of 
last night's candy left!

00:05:18.310 --> 00:05:19.420
There's a lot.

00:05:19.650 --> 00:05:21.270
Let's bring drinks, too.

00:05:21.270 --> 00:05:22.560
I need my camera...

00:05:30.790 --> 00:05:33.530
Wow! It's the Milky Way!

00:05:33.530 --> 00:05:36.190
It's my first time seeing it so clearly.

00:05:36.980 --> 00:05:39.150
Oh, Mars is incredible!

00:05:39.150 --> 00:05:41.730
It's in close approach this year, too.

00:05:42.180 --> 00:05:45.230
I think it's about -2, maybe -5 right now?

00:05:45.740 --> 00:05:47.070
Oh, a shooting star!

00:05:47.440 --> 00:05:48.260
Ao!

00:05:48.260 --> 00:05:49.050
Yeah!

00:06:08.930 --> 00:06:10.370
I see.

00:06:10.370 --> 00:06:13.970
So after the Shiny Challenge is over, 
it's straight to entrance exams for you.

00:06:13.970 --> 00:06:17.520
I <i>am</i> taking them this year, 
so I have summer courses.

00:06:18.140 --> 00:06:20.100
What about you, Miracchi and Aocchi?

00:06:20.100 --> 00:06:21.020
Huh?

00:06:21.020 --> 00:06:23.920
What are you going to do after 
you discover an asteroid?

00:06:23.920 --> 00:06:27.530
Are you going to try to become 
researchers like Hanashima-san?

00:06:29.810 --> 00:06:32.770
Our senpai asked us the same thing once,

00:06:32.770 --> 00:06:34.810
but I don't know yet.

00:06:35.330 --> 00:06:39.410
I want to discover an 
asteroid and name it Ao.

00:06:39.910 --> 00:06:41.960
If we're able to make that dream come true,

00:06:42.370 --> 00:06:46.470
then maybe I'll come up with a new dream.

00:06:48.410 --> 00:06:50.180
I see.

00:06:50.180 --> 00:06:53.320
You're taking it one day at a time!

00:06:53.700 --> 00:06:57.060
You both do seem to live in the present.

00:06:57.850 --> 00:06:58.730
Yeah.

00:06:59.090 --> 00:07:00.820
Let's do our best to find one!

00:07:00.820 --> 00:07:04.770
It'd feel great to help you two 
achieve your dream, after all.

00:07:04.770 --> 00:07:06.570
Yeah, let's do our best.

00:07:08.560 --> 00:07:11.470
<i>We've finally made it this far.</i>

00:07:14.850 --> 00:07:17.580
The first round of photographs is done.

00:07:18.010 --> 00:07:20.920
Let's do our best to cover 
yesterday's share, too.

00:07:20.920 --> 00:07:22.080
Right!

00:07:23.570 --> 00:07:25.960
There are more stars than last night.

00:07:25.960 --> 00:07:26.750
Yeah.

00:07:30.720 --> 00:07:33.380
Doesn't look like there are any new ones.

00:07:33.380 --> 00:07:35.170
Send me the data, too!

00:07:35.170 --> 00:07:36.680
I'll double-check it!

00:08:00.260 --> 00:08:02.640
How about this region?

00:08:02.640 --> 00:08:04.960
Nope, nothing.

00:08:05.250 --> 00:08:07.730
Nothing but known ones here.

00:08:07.730 --> 00:08:09.620
Conditions aren't bad tonight,

00:08:09.620 --> 00:08:11.730
so it's up to your luck and tenacity.

00:08:11.730 --> 00:08:13.300
Luck, huh?

00:08:13.560 --> 00:08:17.410
Asteroids are rotating, 
and many are misshapen,

00:08:17.410 --> 00:08:21.080
so they can suddenly seem brighter, 
depending on the angle.

00:08:21.080 --> 00:08:22.270
Mira! Look here!

00:08:22.600 --> 00:08:23.910
It's moving!

00:08:25.040 --> 00:08:25.930
Which one?

00:08:25.930 --> 00:08:26.800
Right here.

00:08:27.390 --> 00:08:28.600
You're right.

00:08:28.600 --> 00:08:31.740
The image is unclear, 
so the auto-discovery isn't reading it...

00:08:32.020 --> 00:08:34.990
There's a chance it could just be noise, too.

00:08:34.990 --> 00:08:38.490
Hanashima-san, just to be sure, 
can you try updating our database

00:08:38.490 --> 00:08:40.340
of known astronomical objects?

00:08:40.340 --> 00:08:41.490
Right.

00:08:43.700 --> 00:08:44.660
<i>Ao...</i>

00:08:45.570 --> 00:08:47.520
Well, what shall we do?

00:08:47.980 --> 00:08:51.570
Shall we photograph the same 
region once more to confirm,

00:08:51.570 --> 00:08:53.580
or try aiming for another region?

00:08:54.010 --> 00:08:57.080
It takes 30 minutes to photograph one region.

00:08:57.080 --> 00:09:00.760
Keeping in mind the time we need to create 
our presentation materials for tomorrow...

00:09:00.760 --> 00:09:04.140
We can only photograph 
one more region tonight.

00:09:04.770 --> 00:09:07.440
Let's photograph the same region again.

00:09:07.440 --> 00:09:10.210
We should place our bets 
on the highest probability.

00:09:10.210 --> 00:09:11.150
Right.

00:09:11.810 --> 00:09:13.650
Tomorin, Makki...

00:09:18.340 --> 00:09:19.570
Please!

00:09:20.500 --> 00:09:21.720
All right.

00:09:30.630 --> 00:09:34.260
Okay, we're done running the initial 
processing on the photograph data.

00:09:34.260 --> 00:09:35.720
It's the moment of truth.

00:09:37.640 --> 00:09:39.700
Hey, let's all push it together.

00:09:39.700 --> 00:09:41.050
Of course!

00:09:42.860 --> 00:09:44.820
Kinda feels like we're a sports team.

00:09:44.820 --> 00:09:46.980
What's wrong with that? 
Let's get our spirits up!

00:09:47.380 --> 00:09:48.940
One, two...

00:09:53.180 --> 00:09:54.860
It's a known object...

00:09:55.280 --> 00:09:56.860
That's a shame.

00:09:57.600 --> 00:10:00.820
Still, now I know it's actually within reach.

00:10:01.400 --> 00:10:05.790
Finding a real asteroid is within our reach.

00:10:05.790 --> 00:10:06.540
Yeah.

00:10:12.960 --> 00:10:16.860
Presenting our results when we 
haven't found anything feels kind of...

00:10:16.860 --> 00:10:19.180
You still need to do the work.

00:10:19.760 --> 00:10:21.790
It's still important to write your reports,

00:10:21.790 --> 00:10:24.270
even if you didn't produce 
the results you hoped for.

00:10:25.620 --> 00:10:28.050
It will become precious information

00:10:28.050 --> 00:10:30.200
for the others observing after you.

00:10:31.770 --> 00:10:32.630
Case in point,

00:10:32.630 --> 00:10:36.520
you made your observations while referencing 
past research data and documentation.

00:10:36.520 --> 00:10:39.740
That's right! Thank you, 
all who came before us!

00:10:40.400 --> 00:10:42.160
Let's do our best, Ao!

00:10:42.160 --> 00:10:42.950
Yeah.

00:10:48.660 --> 00:10:52.380
<b>Murikabushi Telescope</b>

00:10:49.220 --> 00:10:52.380
I think this works for our 
explanation of the telescope.

00:10:52.690 --> 00:10:54.810
Yeah, I think so.

00:10:55.310 --> 00:10:58.600
Oh, I have a picture of the Murikabushi.

00:10:58.600 --> 00:11:02.240
Oh, right. It'll be easier 
to understand with pictures!

00:11:02.240 --> 00:11:04.350
Aocchi, lend me your camera!

00:11:04.350 --> 00:11:04.970
Sure.

00:11:05.350 --> 00:11:07.490
Let's make the pages on 
the observation procedure

00:11:07.490 --> 00:11:09.540
while she does that, Mira-chan.

00:11:09.540 --> 00:11:10.940
Roger that!

00:11:11.290 --> 00:11:13.170
Let's use pictures of the stars, too.

00:11:13.170 --> 00:11:14.150
Good idea!

00:11:22.100 --> 00:11:24.070
The summary pages are done.

00:11:26.960 --> 00:11:29.750
It's already morning...

00:11:30.380 --> 00:11:35.720
All the memos and photos you took 
were a big help, Ao. Thank you.

00:11:35.720 --> 00:11:39.720
No, I should apologize for butting in 
so much when I'm just an observer.

00:11:40.250 --> 00:11:43.300
I had a lot of fun, though!

00:11:44.760 --> 00:11:50.430
Let's have a private discussion 
about your personality, then.

00:11:50.720 --> 00:11:53.440
We give our presentation in a few hours.

00:11:53.440 --> 00:11:54.650
Go to sleep.

00:11:59.170 --> 00:12:02.880
In the end, neither the optical 
group nor the electromagnetic group

00:12:02.880 --> 00:12:05.280
were able to discover any 
new astronomical objects.

00:12:05.850 --> 00:12:09.520
I don't think this experience 
was a waste, though.

00:12:09.990 --> 00:12:11.340
As Hanashima-san told us,

00:12:11.340 --> 00:12:17.380
"This will become precious information 
for the others observing after you."

00:12:18.630 --> 00:12:22.130
We'll make use of our results 
this time around, and one day,

00:12:22.130 --> 00:12:24.750
I'm sure we'll discover 
a new astronomical object.

00:12:25.970 --> 00:12:27.940
Thank you very much!

00:12:41.370 --> 00:12:45.120
Thank you very much for allowing 
me to sit in and watch.

00:12:45.120 --> 00:12:46.910
Thank you very much!

00:12:47.590 --> 00:12:50.160
How was the Shiny Challenge for you?

00:12:50.440 --> 00:12:52.870
I'm glad I came!

00:12:52.870 --> 00:12:54.920
It's made me want to study even more.

00:12:55.520 --> 00:13:01.300
I plan to use what I learned here 
to discover an asteroid someday!

00:13:01.810 --> 00:13:03.750
I'm looking forward to that.

00:13:03.750 --> 00:13:05.260
Please come visit again.

00:13:06.040 --> 00:13:06.990
We will!

00:13:07.760 --> 00:13:12.720
<b>Ishigaki 
Youth 
Center</b>

00:13:08.390 --> 00:13:10.830
If I get into a university near you all,

00:13:10.830 --> 00:13:12.130
I'll come pay you a visit.

00:13:12.130 --> 00:13:12.720
Okay.

00:13:13.010 --> 00:13:14.570
Me, too!

00:13:14.570 --> 00:13:18.560
I'll come visit when I save up enough 
to come down for Neon's tour!

00:13:19.010 --> 00:13:21.920
Please let me stay with you girls when I do!

00:13:21.920 --> 00:13:23.030
Of course!

00:13:23.620 --> 00:13:25.900
Oh, right! I want to take 
a commemorative photo!

00:13:25.900 --> 00:13:27.990
Great idea! Where should we take it?

00:13:28.420 --> 00:13:31.130
A photo? In that case...

00:13:31.130 --> 00:13:32.470
There's only one place.

00:13:32.470 --> 00:13:33.200
Yeah.

00:13:39.060 --> 00:13:41.090
Ready whenever you are.

00:13:42.500 --> 00:13:44.170
One, two...

00:13:47.940 --> 00:13:51.840
<i>That concluded our participation 
in the Shining Star Challenge.</i>

00:13:55.000 --> 00:13:56.980
It went by so quickly.

00:13:56.980 --> 00:13:59.270
Yeah. I had fun, though.

00:14:00.500 --> 00:14:02.980
I'm glad we got to meet Tomorin and Makki.

00:14:03.760 --> 00:14:06.850
It was fun getting to work 
in a team with people

00:14:06.850 --> 00:14:08.670
from completely different fields.

00:14:08.670 --> 00:14:09.520
Yeah.

00:14:09.850 --> 00:14:12.990
In that sense, it's the same 
as the earth sciences club.

00:14:13.490 --> 00:14:17.470
Astronomy and geology,
 and now meteorology, too...

00:14:23.010 --> 00:14:25.650
<i>How did it go on Ishigaki Island?</i>

00:14:25.650 --> 00:14:28.140
It was really fun!

00:14:28.140 --> 00:14:28.800
<i>I see.</i>

00:14:29.270 --> 00:14:30.940
That's great to hear.

00:14:30.940 --> 00:14:35.000
<i>I got to chat with new friends 
under the starry sky.</i>

00:14:35.000 --> 00:14:38.290
<i>Oh, and we even stayed up all night 
preparing our presentation material!</i>

00:14:38.290 --> 00:14:41.060
Sounds like it was a great 
experience for you, Mira.

00:14:45.680 --> 00:14:48.150
When do you think you'll
be coming home next?

00:14:54.170 --> 00:14:56.340
Welcome back, Senpai!

00:14:56.340 --> 00:14:58.330
Welcome back!

00:14:58.560 --> 00:14:59.940
Thanks!

00:14:58.560 --> 00:14:59.940
Thanks.

00:15:01.720 --> 00:15:03.250
Now, you two...

00:15:03.480 --> 00:15:05.460
I'm thinking of a running a special feature

00:15:05.460 --> 00:15:08.510
on the Shining Star Challenge in
 our new issue after summer break.

00:15:08.790 --> 00:15:10.110
A special feature?!

00:15:10.110 --> 00:15:12.760
So please let us interview you soon.

00:15:13.190 --> 00:15:14.540
Interview...

00:15:15.250 --> 00:15:16.920
Hello!

00:15:16.920 --> 00:15:18.180
Hello.

00:15:18.470 --> 00:15:20.360
Suzu-chan, Megu-chan!

00:15:20.360 --> 00:15:22.400
Wow, long time no see!

00:15:22.680 --> 00:15:24.980
It really has been a long time!

00:15:25.300 --> 00:15:28.460
I brought some of Suzuya's 
delicious bread and sweets...

00:15:28.460 --> 00:15:31.360
Wait, huh? There's a bunch of sweets here.

00:15:31.360 --> 00:15:33.680
They're souvenirs from Mira-chan and Ao-san.

00:15:33.680 --> 00:15:36.060
Looks like we're overlapping with them, Onee.

00:15:36.060 --> 00:15:38.870
Suzuya's bread is more than welcome!

00:15:38.870 --> 00:15:40.870
I've been wanting to eat some forever.

00:15:41.270 --> 00:15:44.820
Senpai!

00:15:42.750 --> 00:15:44.220
Hey!

00:15:46.410 --> 00:15:48.550
Thanks for the snacks!

00:15:48.960 --> 00:15:51.850
Onee, I'm heading back to my club now.

00:15:51.850 --> 00:15:53.620
Okay, thanks.

00:15:53.620 --> 00:15:55.800
Oh, Megu-san, here.

00:15:56.580 --> 00:15:57.940
Star sand?

00:15:57.940 --> 00:15:59.540
How cute.

00:15:59.540 --> 00:16:02.560
Stars and sand... Reminds you
 of the earth sciences club.

00:16:02.850 --> 00:16:07.080
Those are actually the shells of 
a creature called Foraminifera.

00:16:07.080 --> 00:16:11.150
Yeah. The older ones are known 
as index fossils or facies fossils.

00:16:11.540 --> 00:16:15.490
They're used to determine the geological
 period and the environment of the time.

00:16:15.810 --> 00:16:18.790
Megu, this is going to take 
a while, so you can go.

00:16:18.790 --> 00:16:22.710
Thank you for the souvenir. See you later!

00:16:23.470 --> 00:16:25.920
This is still incredible.

00:16:26.130 --> 00:16:29.000
I can't believe you collected 
this much in just three days.

00:16:30.450 --> 00:16:34.210
It was a bit challenging, 
but we had Ao helping us, too.

00:16:34.930 --> 00:16:35.900
Um...

00:16:37.090 --> 00:16:38.180
Ao?

00:16:38.980 --> 00:16:40.560
Thank you so much!

00:16:40.910 --> 00:16:44.140
I couldn't have made it to Ishigaki 
Island without all of your help.

00:16:44.810 --> 00:16:47.980
I never could have gone on my own.

00:16:48.880 --> 00:16:51.720
We wanted you both to go, together.

00:16:51.720 --> 00:16:52.520
Yeah.

00:16:52.520 --> 00:16:55.700
Everyone was cheering you on 
during our entire summer camp.

00:16:57.200 --> 00:17:00.160
Oh, thank you for the teruteru bouzu dolls, too!

00:17:00.580 --> 00:17:03.360
That was Nana-chan's idea.

00:17:03.740 --> 00:17:05.170
Huh? Really?!

00:17:05.690 --> 00:17:07.310
Nana-chan, thank you!

00:17:07.310 --> 00:17:08.840
They really helped!

00:17:09.140 --> 00:17:11.300
I didn't do anything...

00:17:11.680 --> 00:17:14.660
Um, I've loaded the photo data.

00:17:14.660 --> 00:17:16.990
Wow, I want to see!

00:17:16.990 --> 00:17:18.260
Me, too!

00:17:24.260 --> 00:17:25.670
So pretty!

00:17:25.670 --> 00:17:28.440
I wish I could have seen this in person.

00:17:29.420 --> 00:17:30.760
It looks like fun...

00:17:32.220 --> 00:17:34.750
These two are Tomorin and Makki.

00:17:34.750 --> 00:17:36.530
They were in our group.

00:17:36.530 --> 00:17:38.360
Tell me more!

00:17:39.540 --> 00:17:41.330
Oh, it's from Tomorin!

00:17:41.940 --> 00:17:43.280
<i>Yoo-hoo!</i>

00:17:43.280 --> 00:17:46.850
<i>Guess what?! I got to meet Neon 
in person at the Star Festival!</i>

00:17:46.850 --> 00:17:49.640
<i>Wow! Good for you, Tomorin!</i>

00:17:49.640 --> 00:17:52.640
<i>I was so happy, my memory's missing in places.</i>

00:17:52.640 --> 00:17:54.230
<i>What?!</i>

00:17:54.230 --> 00:17:58.060
<i>Sounds like temporary amnesia 
caused by extreme stress.</i>

00:18:01.740 --> 00:18:05.730
Hey, why don't we all go stargazing 
for the first time in a while?

00:18:06.150 --> 00:18:07.940
I already got permission for you.

00:18:07.940 --> 00:18:09.030
Sensei!

00:18:09.030 --> 00:18:10.400
How long have you been there?

00:18:11.470 --> 00:18:12.300
Here.

00:18:12.300 --> 00:18:14.110
Thank you so much!

00:18:14.550 --> 00:18:17.600
So she wasn't just the barbecue magistrate.

00:18:17.600 --> 00:18:19.780
I am your advisor, you know.

00:18:28.990 --> 00:18:31.770
We can see better on the roof!

00:18:31.770 --> 00:18:33.000
Yeah.

00:18:33.360 --> 00:18:35.710
All done lining up Jupiter!

00:18:36.120 --> 00:18:39.030
You've gotten quite used 
to doing that, Mira-chan.

00:18:40.460 --> 00:18:41.580
Can I?

00:18:41.580 --> 00:18:42.890
Go ahead.

00:18:43.910 --> 00:18:45.470
It's striped.

00:18:45.470 --> 00:18:48.770
Yeah. Tonight, you can see four of its moons.

00:18:49.680 --> 00:18:52.320
Nana-chan, I want to see, too.

00:18:52.320 --> 00:18:53.060
Sure.

00:18:53.970 --> 00:18:55.280
It's striped.

00:18:57.460 --> 00:18:59.600
Mira-senpai, Ao-senpai.

00:19:00.560 --> 00:19:06.040
U-Um, I'm sorry you couldn't 
discover an asteroid this time...

00:19:06.470 --> 00:19:09.080
but please keep trying!

00:19:10.480 --> 00:19:12.150
Yeah, we will!

00:19:12.150 --> 00:19:13.080
Thank you.

00:19:17.010 --> 00:19:20.550
Looks like Nana-chan's smiling 
more than she used to.

00:19:21.160 --> 00:19:22.600
Now that you mention it...

00:19:22.600 --> 00:19:24.050
That's great.

00:19:24.620 --> 00:19:28.340
It sounds like Chikage's taken an 
interest in astronomy lately, too.

00:19:28.340 --> 00:19:29.770
She has?!

00:19:30.540 --> 00:19:32.770
It was the same for us.

00:19:32.770 --> 00:19:35.870
Being around Sakura-senpai and Ino-senpai

00:19:35.870 --> 00:19:38.500
made us grow interested in geology, as well.

00:19:38.500 --> 00:19:41.720
You're right. So much has happened.

00:21:25.780 --> 00:21:28.960
Everyone has their own things they 
love and their own special talents,

00:21:28.960 --> 00:21:31.310
their own unique world.

00:21:31.730 --> 00:21:34.640
If you're all by yourself, 
you only have your own world,

00:21:34.640 --> 00:21:36.440
but when it connects to others,

00:21:36.440 --> 00:21:39.930
the possibilities spread 
out endlessly before you.

00:21:40.420 --> 00:21:43.230
Just like the vast, unknown

00:21:44.770 --> 00:21:45.950
cosmos.

00:21:52.100 --> 00:21:54.620
Let's discover one someday.

00:21:54.990 --> 00:21:55.870
Yeah.

00:21:59.200 --> 00:22:00.400
Let's discover one.

00:22:01.420 --> 00:22:02.630
And let's fulfill...

00:22:04.350 --> 00:22:05.880
...our...

00:22:07.250 --> 00:22:08.650
...promise together.
